@@ -0,0 +1,53 @@
+---
+id: dl-003-json-merge-patch-rfc-7386-semantics-for-applymergepatch
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Merge Patch RFC 7386 semantics for applyMergePatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 7386 gives a nearly-complete pseudocode algorithm for merge patches, unlike RFC 6902's prose, so
+this feature leaves far fewer open questions than `applyPatch` did. The few it does leave still need
+fixing once, consistently, rather than redecided per caller.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **"Object" means a plain JSON object, not an array**: RFC 7386's algorithm branches on whether
+  `Patch`/`Target` "is an Object". Arrays and objects are both `typeof "object"` in JavaScript, so
+  this is checked explicitly (`typeof value === "object" && value !== null && !Array.isArray(value)`)
+  — matching the RFC's own Appendix A.9 example, where a patch array replaces a target array wholesale
+  instead of being walked member-by-member.
+- **A non-object target is discarded, not merged into**: per the algorithm's `if Target is not an
+  Object: Target = {}`, when `patch` is an object but `target` is a scalar, `null`, or an array, the
+  result starts from an empty object rather than attempting to graft object members onto a
+  non-object value (RFC 7386 Appendix A.14).
+- **Deep-clone on every path, mirroring `applyPatch` (dl-002)**: the target is cloned up front with
+  `structuredClone` before any member is deleted or overwritten, and a patch value that terminates
+  the recursion (anything that is not itself a plain object) is also `structuredClone`d before being
+  placed in the result. This keeps the merged document free of shared mutable references with both
+  `target` and `patch`, the same "must not mutate its input" guarantee `applyPatch` already provides,
+  for the same reason: callers that keep using their original `target`/`patch` values after the call
+  must not observe later mutations of the returned document, or vice versa.
+- **A patch member naming a key absent from the target is a silent no-op**: RFC 7386's pseudocode
+  only removes `Name` from `Target` "if Name exists in Target"; it does not treat a `null` for a
+  missing key as an error. `applyMergePatch` never throws for this case, unlike `applyPatch`'s
+  `remove`/`replace`, which do throw when their target is missing (dl-002) — the two functions are
+  intentionally not symmetric here, because RFC 7386 itself defines merge patches as tolerant of
+  this, with no equivalent to JSON Patch's explicit operation-level preconditions.
+- **No syntactic validation of `patch` is possible or attempted**: unlike JSON Patch, every JSON
+  value is a syntactically valid JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386 §1), so there is no analogue to
+  `validateOperation`. `applyMergePatch` never throws for a malformed `patch`; the only way it throws
+  is the same way `structuredClone` always can, for a `target`/`patch` value that isn't valid JSON
+  (e.g. containing a function or a circular reference) — a boundary case that is this function's
+  responsibility no more than it already is `applyPatch`'s.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers can treat `applyMergePatch` as total over JSON values: there is no "invalid patch" error
+  path to handle, only the merged result.
+- `applyMergePatch` and `applyPatch` deliberately disagree on whether a missing target is an error;
+  callers migrating a patch from one format to the other must not assume identical error behaviour.
+- If a future caller needs RFC 7386-style strict validation that `patch` contains no non-JSON values
+  up front (rather than failing inside `structuredClone`), that must be layered outside
+  `applyMergePatch`, consistent with how `applyPatch` and `resolvePointer` already scope themselves.
